course: dictionary
lesson: 12144
title: Comparable things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Comparable means two things are similar enough that you can put them side by side and compare them fairly. Look for clues such as size, amount, quality or price. «The two schools have comparable numbers of pupils» tells you the numbers are close, so the schools are a fair match.
explain: The word can also mean able to be compared at all. «These results are not comparable» means the results cannot be set side by side, perhaps because they were measured in different ways or with different units.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Try "similar enough to compare" in place of comparable. If the sentence still makes sense, you have it. Often the word is followed by to or with, as in comparable to or comparable with.
fact: Comparable means similar enough to be compared.
fact: «Comparable» can be used for size, amount or quality: «two schools of comparable size».
fact: «Comparable» can also mean able to be compared at all: «These results are not comparable».
fact: In science, a fair test needs comparable conditions, and results are only comparable if the same units and methods are used.
fact: Useful phrases are comparable to and comparable with.
exercise: «The two classes have comparable numbers of children.» What does comparable mean here? | Far apart in distance | Ready to leave | *Similar in amount | Very noisy
exercise: «These results are not comparable, because one test used metres and the other used feet.» Why are the results not comparable? | They are all exactly the same | They are the longest in the book | *They cannot be fairly compared | They were written in pencil
exercise: «The two houses are of comparable size.» Find and copy the word that tells you the houses are similar in size. | two | houses | size | *comparable
test: «The two bags were of comparable weight, so Sam could not tell which was heavier.» What does comparable suggest? | Broken | *Similar enough to be compared | Empty and light | Very heavy
test: Mia says, «Our two shops sell comparable products, so compare the prices.» Why can you compare the prices? | The prices are always the same | Mia likes shopping | *The products are similar enough to compare | The shops are closed
exam: «Only results that use the same units are comparable with each other.» Which word means that results can be fairly set side by side? | Results | Units | Only | *Comparable
